   124	@dataclass(frozen=True)
   125	class PhysicsConfig:
   126	    """The physical constants a run may vary, all defaulting to the frozen set.
   127	
   128	    Every field defaults to the value in ``env/link_budget.py``, so the
   129	    dataclass adds no new numbers to the project.  It exists because two of
   130	    them currently contradict each other (``p⁰`` and ``p_max``) and a test
   131	    or a resolved run has to be able to state a consistent pair explicitly
   132	    rather than editing a frozen constant in place.
   133	    """
   134	
   135	    segment_start_power_w: float = SEGMENT_START_POWER_W
   136	    beam_power_max_w: float = BEAM_POWER_MAX_W
   137	    beam_bandwidth_hz: float = BEAM_BANDWIDTH_HZ
   138	    pa_max_efficiency: float = PA_MAX_EFFICIENCY
   139	    pa_saturation_power_w: float = PA_SATURATION_POWER_W
   140	    rician_k_factor_db: float = RICIAN_K_FACTOR_DB
   141	
   142	    segment_warm_start: str = "uniform-episode-length"
   143	    """How old a link's power segment already is when the episode opens.
   144	
   145	    ⚠ **Not a nicety.**  Without it every episode has ``p(0) = p⁰`` for
   146	    every user — measured at 100.0% of segments before this existed — and
   147	    that is an artefact of the episode boundary, not a property of the
   148	    geometry.  It is the same defect W-04 fixed for the D2 latches by
   149	    priming them before step 0, and the same argument settles it.
   150	
   151	    ``uniform-episode-length`` (**the main arm**): the age is drawn
   152	    ``Uniform{0, …, H−1}``, so step 0 looks like a uniformly random step of
   153	    an ongoing episode.  Parameter-free — it reuses ``H`` — and ``a = 0``
   154	    keeps positive probability, so a genuinely fresh segment still occurs;
   155	    it just stops being certain.
   156	
   157	    ``uniform-segment-length`` (**the frozen sensitivity arm**): the age is
   158	    drawn ``Uniform{0, …, L−1}`` with ``L`` the frozen median segment
   159	    length.  This is the renewal-equilibrium age distribution and is
   160	    theoretically the more correct one, but ``L`` is measured **under the
   161	    reference policy**, which is how a policy gets back into the initial
   162	    state distribution — the reason (d1) was rejected.
   163	
   164	    ⚠ The main arm's bias is **not** conservative.  At ``Δt = 30.08 s`` the
   165	    measured ``L`` is 5 steps against ``H = 10``, so drawing over ``H``
   166	    ages segments *beyond* their typical life and pushes ``p`` further from
   167	    ``p⁰`` — i.e. it makes the mechanism look **more** active, in exactly
   168	    the direction we are trying to establish.  That is why the second arm
   169	    is frozen alongside it rather than discussed: if the two arms disagree
   170	    on a headline, the disagreement is the finding.
   171	
   172	    ``none`` exists only for tests that need ``p(0) = p⁰`` deterministically.
   173	    """
